## Method and reasoning
At index i store the sum of the block of length i & −i ending at i. A prefix query removes its final block repeatedly by subtracting that low bit. A point update walks through every containing block by adding the low bit. Take the difference of two prefixes for a range. Initialization by point updates costs O(n log n), each operation O(log n), space O(n).

## Walk through the sample
ADD 2 7 changes the second element from −1 to 6. SUM 2 3 then subtracts prefix 1 from prefix 3, giving (2 + 6 + 4) − 2 = 10. Negative updates use the same block additions.

## Complexity
The reference builds by inserting n values in O(n log n). Each ADD or SUM costs O(log n); the tree uses O(n) space.

## Reference implementation
~~~
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class Fenwick {
    vector<long long> tree;

  public:
    explicit Fenwick(int n) : tree(n + 1) {}
    void add(int i, long long x) {
        for (; i < int(tree.size()); i += i & -i)
            tree[i] += x;
    }
    long long prefix(int i) const {
        long long sum = 0;
        for (; i > 0; i -= i & -i)
            sum += tree[i];
        return sum;
    }
};
int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    int n, q;
    cin >> n >> q;
    Fenwick tree(n);
    for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) {
        long long x;
        cin >> x;
        tree.add(i, x);
    }
    while (q--) {
        string op;
        int i;
        long long x;
        cin >> op >> i >> x;
        if (op == "ADD")
            tree.add(i, x);
        else
            cout << tree.prefix(x) - tree.prefix(i - 1) << '\n';
    }
}
~~~
